WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.000
Now I’m writing this before the Emmy’s
and if I had to guess they probably gave the

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.120
best drama series to Succession because rightfully
so, it’s an incredible show.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:14.290
But unfortunately, if that is the case, then
that means Better Call Saul has not won best

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:16.420
drama series a single time.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:20.430
And because of how upsetting that statement
is, I wanted to make this video and analyze

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:24.620
why Better Call Saul is the best show of 2023.

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:29.040
So the first and most important reason to
me is that Better Call Saul features an absolutely

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.750
engrossing ensemble cast of characters.

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:35.320
They’re all extremely deep and have intricate
dynamics.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:40.399
It’s honestly really hard to pick a favorite,
but if I was held at gunpoint then I’d probably

00:00:40.399 --> 00:00:42.340
pick Kim Wexler.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:46.640
Everyone in the show I think is outstanding,
but Kim essentially excels past them and is

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:51.260
special to me because there’s a lot of poor
examples of female characters in media.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:55.400
Kim as a character I think is a perfect example
as to how to write a strong, independent,

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.239
and fierce woman.

00:00:57.239 --> 00:01:01.460
The writers imbue Kim with a great amount
of ferocious energy when it comes to handling

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:04.610
conflict that it makes her captivating to
watch.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:09.320
Every time she explodes and eviscerates a
character with only her words, it’s so mesmerizing

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:11.400
because of how smart she is.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:15.540
And this aspect about Kim doesn’t make her
a mean character or anything because she has

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.690
one of the strongest moral consciousnesses
in the show.

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:22.360
Her desire in wanting to make a difference
in the world and help the underdog is a wholly

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:23.880
endearing character trait.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:28.979
She’s like a perfect storm of characteristics
that if written competently makes an excellent

00:01:28.979 --> 00:01:29.979
character.

00:01:29.979 --> 00:01:33.370
Which Vince Gilligan and the other writers
for Better Call Saul do so expertly.

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:37.840
There’s not a single moment in which I thought
Kim Wexler was written poorly or a single

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:39.720
line of dialogue was wasted.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:44.149
And that’s kind of the case for everyone
in the show because of how immaculate and

00:01:44.149 --> 00:01:46.700
detailed the writing is overall.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:50.869
Another aspect that elevates Kim and makes
her even more fun to watch are the characters

00:01:50.869 --> 00:01:52.290
surrounding her.

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:57.329
The general space for law practices and these
big corporations are predominantly male-dominated

00:01:57.329 --> 00:02:00.789
and seeing a female character break through
and trample these characters with nothing

00:02:00.789 --> 00:02:05.610
more than her wit and drive is extremely captivating
and inspiring.

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:10.160
The scene in which Kevin from Mesa Verde is
disappointed in Kim and contemplates firing

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:21.720
her and she actually reverses the entire dynamic
by turning the blame on Kevin was pure excellence.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:29.720
The show of high confidence and willingness
to not back down against people in charge

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:33.170
impressed Kevin so much that he wanted to
keep working with her.

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:37.410
Even the scene where she successfully convinces
Lalo that Jimmy wasn’t lying about the whole

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:39.540
money ordeal was amazing.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:44.370
Jimmy in that scene was floundering and she
actually saves Jimmy and is the one to out-persuade

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:45.370
Lalo.

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:51.230
Her pure wit, intelligence, and brass nature
is infectious and those characteristics combined

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:54.230
with her gender makes her a massive role model.

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:58.430
Like I can honestly just watch a Kim Wexler
spinoff show where she demolishes people in

00:02:58.430 --> 00:02:59.940
court every episode.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:04.099
She’s essentially the people’s champion
and another thing that is pretty refreshing

00:03:04.099 --> 00:03:07.159
is that the writers don’t wave around the
fact that she’s a female.

00:03:07.159 --> 00:03:11.670
They’re solely focused on creating a compelling
character because at the end of the day, whether

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:15.730
you’re a guy or girl you have your own set
of strengths and flaws.

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:19.800
And one of my favorite moments for Kim as
a character was a scene towards the end of

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:25.560
the show where all of her emotions essentially
boil over and she breaks down in public.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:30.019
Throughout the show, she’s had such a strong
exterior and rarely showed a lot of vulnerability

00:03:30.019 --> 00:03:31.019
emotionally.

00:03:31.019 --> 00:03:35.629
However in this moment, since she’s essentially
dredging up the horrific past of Howard’s

00:03:35.629 --> 00:03:40.090
demise, and she isn’t burying it alongside
Jimmy and acting like it didn’t happen,

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:42.239
she can’t help but break down and cry.

00:03:42.239 --> 00:03:46.439
It’s a deeply personal and moving moment
because it’s such a convention break for

00:03:46.439 --> 00:03:51.330
the character and if anything makes her even
more relatable and human.

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:55.610
Now let’s go into the relationship of Jimmy
and Kim because I honestly think this is one

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:58.480
of the best relationship dynamics that I’ve
ever seen.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:03.250
I can’t really think of a lot that can compete
because there’s such an intriguing counterbalance

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:06.040
dynamic to Jimmy and Kim’s relationship.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:10.470
It funnily enough is like the scales of justice
icon in which when Kim and Jimmy are together

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:13.500
they are at an equilibrium point for each
other.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:18.340
Now this obviously isn’t a good equilibrium,
but it essentially enables Kim’s worst impulses

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:22.070
while also tempering Jimmy’s and keeping
him in check.

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:26.580
Jimmy when in the presence of Kim wants to
be the best version of himself so that she’ll

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:27.590
love him more.

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:31.169
Seeing him grapple with the conundrum of whether
or not he should tell her the truth about

00:04:31.169 --> 00:04:36.730
what happened in the desert was heart-wrenching
because this moment could lead to their breakup.

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:40.860
But it in fact actually strengthened their
relationship because Jimmy told her the truth

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:43.770
and his core character wasn’t tarnished
by it.

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:48.410
Then for Kim, she has a deep-seated exposure
to criminal behavior through her mother.

00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:52.150
The show is kind of vague as to how much this
impacted her, but it makes sense to get a

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:56.910
rush from doing things that are slightly out
of the law as long as they aren’t too far.

00:04:56.910 --> 00:05:01.060
So when around Jimmy she can’t help herself
because it’s euphoric for her to execute

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:04.470
schemes with Jimmy that always pay off magnificently.

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:08.430
When together they balance each other out
to an exact morality and through their witty

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:12.810
expertise and newfound grey area, they’re
unstoppable.

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:16.990
Like them teaming up to tank Howard's character
was one of the best arcs in television and

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:21.110
had a wildly insane payoff that had me dying
of laughter throughout.

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:25.490
However, when Kim and Jimmy aren’t together
the scales shift for the better for Kim and

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:26.840
for the worse for Jimmy.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:31.240
I love how they explore this story-wise because
when Kim leaves Jimmy she defaults to the

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:35.660
most stereotypical vanilla good life, while
Jimmy goes off the deep end and risks more

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:37.130
and more for money.

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:41.810
This duality is such a juicy juxtaposing relationship
that it is oddly alluring.

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:46.360
And because of this, it makes for an incredibly
engaging character pairing.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:50.470
That and the way they left things between
Kim and Jimmy was perfect and really every

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:54.380
ending for each of the characters goes above
and beyond for great writing.

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:59.810
Shifting my focus over to Jimmy now, what
the show does for him is pretty incredible.

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:04.050
They make one of the most complex characters
in the Breaking Bad universe that has years

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:08.699
of backstory that weaves into every nanosecond
of his character’s journey.

00:06:08.699 --> 00:06:13.650
Whether that be his complicated relationship
with Chuck, Kim anchoring him down, his criminal

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:18.690
past as slippin' Jimmy, his outlook on life
in which there are only wolves and sheep,

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:23.220
and his main goal in life being the pursuit
of riches makes a delicious hot pot combination

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:24.380
of a character.

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:27.620
To me, he’s just as interesting as Walter
White.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:32.210
Now Walter’s overall all arc I think is
the magnum opus of character arcs in television

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:37.060
period, but I think Jimmy as a character rivals
Walter because he’s just as rich.

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:38.840
And I don’t mean that literally.

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:43.140
There’s a ton of different scenes and aspects
in which you can analyze and dive into for

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:48.039
his character, but to stay within season 6,
I absolutely love how the creators handled

00:06:48.039 --> 00:06:49.130
him for the finale.

00:06:49.130 --> 00:06:53.419
They essentially establish a character flaw
through flashbacks in which Jimmy states he

00:06:53.419 --> 00:06:55.010
has no regrets in life.

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:59.150
I love how they also do this with Mike and
Walter because they really question Jimmy

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:01.129
about the time machine question.

00:07:01.129 --> 00:07:05.030
Like Jimmy only wanting to go back in the
past to either make money or fix something

00:07:05.030 --> 00:07:06.910
as silly as an injury.

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:21.900
But since we know Jimmy’s past through the
show, we know he’s lying to himself.

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:26.080
So the ending arc for Jimmy is that he wants
to redeem himself and rectify his biggest

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:30.090
regret in life, and that’s to make amends
with his true love Kim.

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:35.590
The way in which Vince Gilligan explores this
path for the finale I think is utterly brilliant.

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:39.710
Every scene is actually a building block element
to get to Jimmy’s redemption despite it

00:07:39.710 --> 00:07:41.470
not being obvious.

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:45.690
Like the flashbacks establishing the flaw,
the present where Jimmy rolls his opposition

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:50.970
by getting a 7 year plea deal, which was genius
because he essentially hijacks Marie’s victimhood

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:55.621
testimony and throws himself into that pool
by being a victim of Walter as well, then

00:07:55.621 --> 00:08:00.370
to him figuring out that Kim confessed about
Howard and is able to bring her to the court,

00:08:00.370 --> 00:08:04.900
which sparks a revelation in which he can
rectify his biggest regret, to the explosive

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:09.150
climax of Jimmy admitting he did everything
for money to vindicate himself in the eyes

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:13.900
of Kim and also the law, and then the cherry
on top with him and Chuck in which we learn

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:16.740
that Jimmy always loved Chuck deep down.

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:22.110
To me this is a perfect example of not a single
wasted scene or moment, that builds to an

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:23.360
outstanding ending.

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:27.210
It’s yet another magnum opus ending within
the breaking bad universe.

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:31.970
It’s also pretty insane because we both
get the satisfaction of Jimmy destroying his

00:08:31.970 --> 00:08:37.640
opposition in court by getting a 7 year plea
deal, but he also does the right thing character-wise

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:40.510
by burning it all down to redeem himself.

00:08:40.510 --> 00:08:44.760
This overall tug-of-war between Jimmy’s
criminal instincts and his innocent aura of

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:48.950
love with Kim is one of the best complexities
a character has ever had.

00:08:48.950 --> 00:08:53.370
It makes Jimmy a perfect candidate for the
main character of the series because there’s

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:58.300
always a tonne of under-the-surface motivations,
desires, and goals that makes him one of the

00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:02.090
most intriguing characters in television.

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:05.839
Moving over to Mike since he’s also kind
of the second main character of the series

00:09:05.839 --> 00:09:11.020
due to him being the protagonist of the drug
war plotline, he’s obviously amazing.

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:15.740
Mike has always been a fan favorite because
of his lovable and honorable personality.

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:20.200
Better Call Saul perfectly utilizes him and
even has some incredible arcs for him.

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:25.339
Even though it isn’t in season 6, his season
5 arc with Werner Ziegler was outstanding.

00:09:25.339 --> 00:09:30.110
At first, I thought this whole development
was rather uninteresting about the whole backstory

00:09:30.110 --> 00:09:34.440
of the creation of the underground lab, but
the second they imbue this with a heart-wrenching

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:38.520
conflict of Werner defecting, it becomes magnetizing.

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:42.779
Mike having to put him down is one of the
biggest tragedies in the show because Werner

00:09:42.779 --> 00:09:45.510
is essentially the embodiment of innocence.

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:49.470
And that’s a line that Mike never wants
to cross, but he knows that he is the lesser

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:51.020
evil for killing Werner.

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:55.390
It’s one of the most gutting scenes in the
show and is emotionally wrecking because Mike

00:09:55.390 --> 00:09:57.400
has never been this torn apart emotionally.

00:09:57.400 --> 00:10:01.470
And the second Werner realizes his fate your
heart sinks.

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:05.589
He doesn’t even try to fight for his life,
and all he wanted to do was ensure the safety

00:10:05.589 --> 00:10:07.240
of his wife.

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:11.610
Anytime this type of story situation happens
it’s always gutting like in Of Mice and

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:13.360
Men and even Old Yeller.

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:17.680
It’s a free ticket to make you sob, and
the way it’s executed here does so much

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:19.260
for Mike as a character.

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:24.300
Mike’s season 6 time isn’t as great since
the main focus is on Gus and Lalo, but this

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:28.120
season actually has one of my favorite Mike
scenes in which he meets Kim.

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:31.970
It’s a legendary pairing that sees the two
most hardened and witty characters have a

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:35.630
conversation and that alone is just so entrancing.

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:39.680
I wish it could have been longer, but Mike’s
observation that Kim was stronger than Jimmy

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:41.620
was such a gratifying moment.

00:10:41.620 --> 00:10:45.390
I’d love to talk about Mike for hours, but
let’s move over to Nacho who I thought was

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:47.070
a major surprise.

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:51.620
Nacho as a character is fundamentally lovable
because he’s a victim who desperately wants

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:53.630
to escape to a better life.

00:10:53.630 --> 00:10:58.779
Which he can do, but since his father is threatened
with death, he chooses the selfless path of

00:10:58.779 --> 00:11:01.360
doing everything in the interest of his safety.

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:05.750
It creates an endearing quality for the character
in which we just want the best for Nacho.

00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:10.480
He’s a crucial perspective within the world
of Better Call Saul because he’s at his

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:11.529
core an underdog.

00:11:11.529 --> 00:11:15.790
We’re always rooting for Nacho to succeed,
but given the nature of the show in which

00:11:15.790 --> 00:11:22.070
anyone in the game receives major consequences,
it turns Nacho’s overall story into a tragedy.

00:11:22.070 --> 00:11:27.530
His ending like everyone in the show is flawless
and is imbued with an extremely raw and intense

00:11:27.530 --> 00:11:28.530
performance.

00:11:28.530 --> 00:11:33.020
Along with his entire journey up until that
point being a crazily gripping ride throughout.

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:37.930
Michael Mando is an excellent gem of an actor
who goes all out in every scene he’s in.

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:43.139
I loved him all the way back in Far Cry 3
as Vaas and I hope he can get more work in

00:11:43.139 --> 00:11:44.139
the future.

00:11:44.139 --> 00:11:47.840
But regardless, his rendition of Nacho will
probably always be his best work and due to

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:52.380
there being so many other great characters
and actors in Better Call Saul, I think he’ll

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:54.570
sadly be overlooked.

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:58.190
Especially given the amount of competition
at the Emmy’s because he didn’t even get

00:11:58.190 --> 00:11:59.190
nominated.

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:03.709
Which feels like a crime, but to be fair the
competition is insane this year.

00:12:03.709 --> 00:12:07.019
Although The White Lotus got a little too
many nominations.

00:12:07.019 --> 00:12:12.120
So Nacho to me will always be a favorite because
of how surprisingly well-acted, written, and

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:14.740
emotionally investing his entire story was.

00:12:14.740 --> 00:12:19.640
He’s essentially the beating heart that
carries the entire drug war plotline.

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:23.860
Then within the sphere of the drug world of
Better Call Saul, we have an amazing addition

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:26.230
to the Salamanca family in Lalo.

00:12:26.230 --> 00:12:31.519
It’s pretty crazy how the Salamancas already
had so many great characters like Tuco, Hector,

00:12:31.519 --> 00:12:36.380
the Twins, but yet, Vince Gilligan somehow
creates the most intriguing member of the

00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:38.060
family for Better Call Saul.

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:43.970
Lalo is a powerful figure with high intelligence,
charisma, is cunning, patient, and my favorite

00:12:43.970 --> 00:12:49.830
aspect about Lalo is that he offsets his ferocity
with an overwhelming presence of kindness.

00:12:49.830 --> 00:12:53.639
It creates an unnerving presence for the character
because he gets under your skin with this

00:12:53.639 --> 00:12:58.389
fake charade of warmth, that he then uses
to catch characters off-guard with his deadly

00:12:58.389 --> 00:12:59.649
seriousness.

00:12:59.649 --> 00:13:04.000
Any time he gives off this fake smile it creates
such a captivating scenario in which you are

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:07.120
trying to interpret what the character is
actually thinking.

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:12.139
It adds a lot of depth to Lalo in any given
moment because he’s a perpetual enigma.

00:13:12.139 --> 00:13:16.740
Which in turn highlights how great both the
writing and acting are for Lalo as a character.

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:21.000
Any confrontation he has with an opposing
character is absolutely riveting because his

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:24.180
unpredictability makes him even more terrifying.

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:28.720
Like whenever he has conversations with Jimmy
and even Kim, you never know the direction

00:13:28.720 --> 00:13:29.850
it’s going to go.

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:34.050
I’d love to go more in-depth about what
makes Lalo such a great villain, but that

00:13:34.050 --> 00:13:37.139
would turn this already long video essay into
feature length.

00:13:37.139 --> 00:13:41.050
He’s hands down an outstanding villain and
it’s really impressive how he was able to

00:13:41.050 --> 00:13:45.290
break through the already magnificent roster
of iconic villains.

00:13:45.290 --> 00:13:48.440
Although I wouldn’t say he’s the best
because I think that title definitely goes

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:49.440
to Gus Fring.

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:54.290
I really love how Better Call Saul adds a
significant amount of detail to Gus and fleshes

00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:55.910
him out even more.

00:13:55.910 --> 00:14:00.220
Seeing the more personal side of Gus gives
viewers more context to the iconic villain

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:02.720
that we didn’t get to see a lot of in Breaking
Bad.

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:07.290
We learn more details about his character
like his exacting and OCD-like personality

00:14:07.290 --> 00:14:09.660
that leads to him being very observant.

00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:13.410
The way in which this comes through the most
is how he analyzes people with his cold and

00:14:13.410 --> 00:14:14.529
unnerving stare.

00:14:14.529 --> 00:14:18.000
I’ll go into more about this through the
subtle and detailed acting portion of the

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:23.470
video because any scene with Gus constantly
oozes subtextual value for storytelling.

00:14:23.470 --> 00:14:26.060
And that’s all done through his immaculate
performance.

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:30.300
He’s a character that requires an exacting
attention to detail because there are so many

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:33.760
intricacies that are constantly telling the
audience what he’s thinking.

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:38.060
He’s also arguably the smartest character
in the Breaking Bad universe, probably just

00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:42.610
barely under Walter, but this makes his entire
story thoroughly intriguing.

00:14:42.610 --> 00:14:47.160
His overall war with Lalo was submerged in
immense tension, and the way in which Gus

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:49.810
overcomes it was a stroke of brilliance.

00:14:49.810 --> 00:14:54.070
This also makes him a better character because
in Breaking Bad he primarily loses to Walter

00:14:54.070 --> 00:14:55.470
on multiple fronts.

00:14:55.470 --> 00:15:00.131
But in Better Call Saul, it brings back the
mastermind aspect of Gus Fring and shows him

00:15:00.131 --> 00:15:02.660
in his full power besting an opponent.

00:15:02.660 --> 00:15:05.300
Thus making him even more deadly and terrifying.

00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:10.199
Which the way he does defeat Lalo was like
a Jigsaw level galaxy brain play in which

00:15:10.199 --> 00:15:15.230
he predicted and accounted an exact outcome
in which he could be brought down to his lab.

00:15:15.230 --> 00:15:20.860
And every step of the way, Gus made it very
convincing that Lalo had won, when in reality,

00:15:20.860 --> 00:15:23.009
Gus was always in control.

00:15:23.009 --> 00:15:26.889
So Better Call Saul actually makes Gus an
even greater villain because we get to see

00:15:26.889 --> 00:15:31.699
him overcome more conflict in his prime, while
also getting much more detailed characteristics

00:15:31.699 --> 00:15:33.089
for the character.

00:15:33.089 --> 00:15:37.589
Just a perfect way to handle Gus that obviously
isn’t done in any kind of nostalgia baity

00:15:37.589 --> 00:15:38.589
way.

00:15:38.589 --> 00:15:42.180
He serves an integral function to the story
and isn’t just here because he was a fan

00:15:42.180 --> 00:15:43.440
favorite villain.

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:48.050
Which a lot of times when fan favorite characters
return in popular franchises it’s really

00:15:48.050 --> 00:15:52.430
only done to make money, while here it actually
elevates Gus.

00:15:52.430 --> 00:15:56.620
Now going into what I would say is my least
favorite character, we have Howard.

00:15:56.620 --> 00:16:02.040
And that’s literally only because his competition
is so insane that by virtue he’s my least

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:03.040
favorite.

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:06.880
Which isn’t to say he’s a bad character
because he’s still fantastic and I love

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:07.880
him.

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:12.470
And if anything, the ending of Howard excels
him into a legendary status because I’d

00:16:12.470 --> 00:16:16.690
honestly make the argument that Howard may
have the best ending in Better Call Saul.

00:16:16.690 --> 00:16:21.149
And that isn’t just because his ending is
shocking, and instead, it’s the tragedy

00:16:21.149 --> 00:16:22.199
of it all.

00:16:22.199 --> 00:16:25.990
This moment in the story absolutely floors
you because you’d never assume a character

00:16:25.990 --> 00:16:28.560
like Howard to be entangled by the cartel.

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:32.880
But heyyy, actions have consequences and I
think this is also a perfect way to really

00:16:32.880 --> 00:16:34.029
punish Kim and Jimmy.

00:16:34.029 --> 00:16:38.060
Initially, I thought there could be a possibility
of Howard killing himself in front of Jimmy

00:16:38.060 --> 00:16:42.649
and Kim which would kind of mirror Chuck’s
suicide ending, but that was immediately swatted

00:16:42.649 --> 00:16:46.449
away with the introduction of the candle,
which I’ll talk about later in the hidden

00:16:46.449 --> 00:16:48.560
meaning section of the video.

00:16:48.560 --> 00:16:52.431
Because this scene in general I think is a
complete masterclass in filmmaking and I want

00:16:52.431 --> 00:16:53.970
to analyze it further.

00:16:53.970 --> 00:16:58.260
But going back to Howard’s death, I think
Lalo abruptly killing him is infinitely more

00:16:58.260 --> 00:17:02.750
tragic than a suicide ending because this
tragedy was forced onto Howard.

00:17:02.750 --> 00:17:06.709
Right when he states that he is going to overcome
his shortcomings he dies.

00:17:06.709 --> 00:17:11.030
He was going to become a better person and
fight the toxicity of Jimmy and Kim’s powerful

00:17:11.030 --> 00:17:15.589
duo, but sadly that optimistic future was
ripped away from him.

00:17:15.589 --> 00:17:19.569
That and the implications of what this does
to Howard’s character overall is even more

00:17:19.569 --> 00:17:24.089
depressing because now all that people will
remember him as is a coke addict who killed

00:17:24.089 --> 00:17:25.089
himself.

00:17:25.089 --> 00:17:29.510
It yet again means that Jimmy and Kim destroys
the legacy of a character, although Kim didn’t

00:17:29.510 --> 00:17:31.980
really have that much involvement with Chuck’s
downfall.

00:17:31.980 --> 00:17:37.061
However, Howards is more tragic to me because
it not only tarnishes his reputation, but

00:17:37.061 --> 00:17:41.780
he was also an innocent character who was
unfortunately at the wrong place at the wrong

00:17:41.780 --> 00:17:42.780
time.

00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:46.720
So in general, the creators use Howard's death
to really punctuate the terrible ways in which

00:17:46.720 --> 00:17:48.330
Jim and Kimmy affect people.

00:17:48.330 --> 00:17:52.360
His character kind of ascends and fulfills
a higher story purpose, and that’s something

00:17:52.360 --> 00:17:55.909
you never would have expected to happen with
a character like Howard.

00:17:55.909 --> 00:17:59.429
Now before going into the perfect prequel
portion of the video, I feel like it would

00:17:59.429 --> 00:18:02.150
be a disservice to the show if I didn’t
mention Chuck.

00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:05.860
I’m trying to base this video around the
final season of the show and why it’s the

00:18:05.860 --> 00:18:11.580
best season of television in 2023 while also
being an overall analysis, but Chuck is inherently

00:18:11.580 --> 00:18:13.590
an important part of Better Call Saul.

00:18:13.590 --> 00:18:18.440
His relationship and overall feud with Jimmy
had a magnificent arc of plenty of ups and

00:18:18.440 --> 00:18:22.860
downs throughout the 4 seasons and one of
the best moments is easily his court monologue

00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:24.000
ranting about Jimmy.

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:28.289
It’s an excellent and euphoric moment of
payoff since Chuck has always held this hate

00:18:28.289 --> 00:18:31.620
under the surface and never expressed it out
loud.

00:18:31.620 --> 00:18:34.110
Unless it was straight to Jimmy’s face.

00:18:34.110 --> 00:18:38.240
But finally hearing this hate pour out in
public was like popping a massive pimple and

00:18:38.240 --> 00:18:39.809
watching all the puss pour out.

00:18:39.809 --> 00:18:44.330
There’s a lot to be analyzed between Chuck
and Jimmy that could also span another big

00:18:44.330 --> 00:18:48.840
video essay, but one aspect that really intrigues
me is the subjective lens in which we view

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:50.390
Chuck as a character.

00:18:50.390 --> 00:18:54.930
Because in the show objectively, Chuck is
a beloved character who has done so much good

00:18:54.930 --> 00:18:58.900
in his career, but yet, the writers only show
us the bad side of Chuck.

00:18:58.900 --> 00:19:04.049
A spiteful, revenge fueled brother that will
never drop his prejudice against Jimmy.

00:19:04.049 --> 00:19:08.480
Due to this subjective lens, it rallies us
behind Jimmy and shows us the true nature

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:09.480
of Chuck.

00:19:09.480 --> 00:19:13.201
Were not shown the great side of all his good
deeds and most of his flashbacks generally

00:19:13.201 --> 00:19:15.299
show him in a bad light.

00:19:15.299 --> 00:19:19.940
And I also love how by orientation to Jimmy,
Chuck is actually the antagonist of the first

00:19:19.940 --> 00:19:20.940
4 seasons.

00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:25.450
Chuck causes most of the conflict that Jimmy
overcomes and it just creates such an interesting

00:19:25.450 --> 00:19:28.540
dynamic because on paper Chuck should be a
hero.

00:19:28.540 --> 00:19:32.830
It establishes a notion that despite how good
you are you can still have deep character

00:19:32.830 --> 00:19:36.049
flaws and that makes Chuck feel so human.

00:19:36.049 --> 00:19:40.640
Vince Gilligan is an utter genius for creating
such an insanely rich roster of characters

00:19:40.640 --> 00:19:43.460
that I can honestly keep gushing about.

00:19:43.460 --> 00:19:46.659
But there are still four other categories
that excel Better Call Saul into being the

00:19:46.659 --> 00:19:49.160
best show of 2023.

00:19:49.160 --> 00:19:52.780
Prequels in general can be very hard because
you can fall into the trap of creating an

00:19:52.780 --> 00:19:57.100
uninteresting story from the audience already
knowing the ending of the main plot.

00:19:57.100 --> 00:20:01.270
So there’s a lot of aspects you have to
account for to make a prequel just as great

00:20:01.270 --> 00:20:02.299
as the original.

00:20:02.299 --> 00:20:05.610
The first aspect is the choice to focus the
narrative on Jimmy.

00:20:05.610 --> 00:20:09.720
If this was a prequel of Walter White as an
example, it wouldn’t be as investing because

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:13.799
we already know the set of events with Elliot
and the company Walter left.

00:20:13.799 --> 00:20:16.520
Along with there not being much left to learn
about Walter as a character.

00:20:16.520 --> 00:20:20.929
Through the course of Breaking Bad, the amount
of backstory we get for Saul Goodman is pretty

00:20:20.929 --> 00:20:21.929
thin.

00:20:21.929 --> 00:20:25.860
We understand Saul Goodman, but there’s
a lot of background context that can be added

00:20:25.860 --> 00:20:27.249
to this character.

00:20:27.249 --> 00:20:33.120
So in general, this show operates to fundamentally
expand and fully flesh out Jimmy as a character.

00:20:33.120 --> 00:20:38.050
Great prequels add to the characters at hand
and any mediocre prequel that tackles pre-established

00:20:38.050 --> 00:20:40.890
characters are never really that intriguing.

00:20:40.890 --> 00:20:45.580
Because they lack exciting character development
and oftentimes communicate redundant information

00:20:45.580 --> 00:20:48.350
that was already communicated in the original.

00:20:48.350 --> 00:20:52.970
That and despite the show being called Better
Call Saul, Mike also acts as a dual lead in

00:20:52.970 --> 00:20:56.510
the narrative because he has an entire plotline
dedicated to him.

00:20:56.510 --> 00:21:00.700
Now I wouldn’t say that Mike needed a lot
more development character-wise because his

00:21:00.700 --> 00:21:05.620
motivations are relatively simple and his
personality is mostly the same.

00:21:05.620 --> 00:21:09.610
However, the main interesting question for
Mike is how he ended up working with Gus and

00:21:09.610 --> 00:21:10.610
Jimmy.

00:21:10.610 --> 00:21:14.520
That’s a major motivator in capturing the
audience’s interest because for a prequel

00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:17.950
you also need to tell a story that will thoroughly
entertain the audience.

00:21:17.950 --> 00:21:21.660
And this aspect of the story is inherently
a mystery in Breaking Bad.

00:21:21.660 --> 00:21:26.110
What the writers came up with for these two
leads was insanely captivating while also

00:21:26.110 --> 00:21:29.260
greatly expanding upon the world of Breaking
Bad.

00:21:29.260 --> 00:21:32.210
Because that’s another thing that every
prequel needs to do.

00:21:32.210 --> 00:21:36.890
If you aren’t exploring anything new and
are mostly retreading old waters, then you

00:21:36.890 --> 00:21:39.600
are doing a disservice to the world you’re
telling your story in.

00:21:39.600 --> 00:21:42.780
You have to introduce new elements into the
narrative that the audience don’t know the

00:21:42.780 --> 00:21:44.350
resolution of.

00:21:44.350 --> 00:21:48.800
Like examples being Nacho, Lalo, Howard, Chuck,
and Kim.

00:21:48.800 --> 00:21:53.000
These are all characters that are new to us
and thus we don’t know their endings.

00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:56.760
The only character out of this roster that
you’ll know the ending of is Lalo because

00:21:56.760 --> 00:22:02.420
he’s obviously not in Breaking Bad and his
opponent, Gus Fring, survives their conflict.

00:22:02.420 --> 00:22:07.370
However, the outcome of this conflict isn’t
the alluring part of the story, and instead,

00:22:07.370 --> 00:22:10.860
it’s the way in which Gus overcomes Lalo
as a villain.

00:22:10.860 --> 00:22:16.170
Their tug of war of conflict throughout seasons
5 and 6 was incredible and features some of

00:22:16.170 --> 00:22:19.000
the most witty and engaging mindgames.

00:22:19.000 --> 00:22:24.250
This is a complicated game of chess, and despite
us knowing that Gus wins that game of chess,

00:22:24.250 --> 00:22:26.270
the way it’s played is deeply riveting.

00:22:26.270 --> 00:22:30.650
And even though we can infer the ending of
Lalo’s character, we don’t know how he’ll

00:22:30.650 --> 00:22:33.080
impact other characters like Jimmy and Kim.

00:22:33.080 --> 00:22:36.950
Our only assurance is that Jimmy survives
to Breaking Bad, but the fate of Kim is in

00:22:36.950 --> 00:22:40.230
question and that in turn makes us fear for
her safety.

00:22:40.230 --> 00:22:44.380
Which this is something that prequels need
because, without this, it can lead to shallow

00:22:44.380 --> 00:22:48.320
stakes from us knowing the outcome of every
part of the story.

00:22:48.320 --> 00:22:52.770
Stakes and relative uncertainty as to where
the story is leading is what hooks the audience

00:22:52.770 --> 00:22:55.150
and makes them want to keep watching.

00:22:55.150 --> 00:22:59.480
Better Call Saul supplies this in every season
and essentially gets to a point in seasons

00:22:59.480 --> 00:23:03.450
5 and 6 where it even matches the quality
of Breaking Bad.

00:23:03.450 --> 00:23:07.779
Like the resolutions of Nacho and Howard were
sublime and these kind of moments being in

00:23:07.779 --> 00:23:12.330
a prequel of all things is insanely impressive.

00:23:12.330 --> 00:23:16.610
Given how hard it is to create unpredictable
plot threads in a prequel story.

00:23:16.610 --> 00:23:21.549
Then one more aspect about Better Call Saul
is that it’s also a sequel to Breaking Bad.

00:23:21.549 --> 00:23:25.929
So not only does it add a lot of context to
the story and characters in the past, it also

00:23:25.929 --> 00:23:29.529
gives extended resolutions to existing characters.

00:23:29.529 --> 00:23:31.830
Like one of the main ones being Marie Schrader.

00:23:31.830 --> 00:23:37.059
Her testimony against Jimmy was deeply emotional
and she utterly rails into him.

00:23:37.059 --> 00:23:42.380
It gives her character more of a satisfactory
end because she gets her justice against Jimmy.

00:23:42.380 --> 00:23:46.270
And more than just him going to jail because
she got to confront him.

00:23:46.270 --> 00:23:50.789
Then for Jimmy, the creators could have left
Saul Goodman’s end rather ambiguous like

00:23:50.789 --> 00:23:54.580
Jesse’s, but it’s an inherent upgrade
than him just being in hiding for the rest

00:23:54.580 --> 00:23:55.790
of his life.

00:23:55.790 --> 00:24:00.390
Like sure, you can go that direction for the
story, but it makes for a stronger conclusion

00:24:00.390 --> 00:24:04.520
to continue it and create a better payoff
for Jimmy as a character in which he gets

00:24:04.520 --> 00:24:05.640
redemption.

00:24:05.640 --> 00:24:09.810
The fact that Better Call Saul is able to
continue the story and create another ending

00:24:09.810 --> 00:24:13.799
that goes above perfection is mindboggling
to me.

00:24:13.799 --> 00:24:17.200
Vince Gilligan and his creative team are just
geniuses.

00:24:17.200 --> 00:24:21.320
So Better Call Saul as a show is more than
just a perfect prequel, but ascends the story

00:24:21.320 --> 00:24:24.330
foundations of prequels and does even more.

00:24:24.330 --> 00:24:28.659
And before moving on, I find it really interesting
how they use the black-and-white style for

00:24:28.659 --> 00:24:32.149
the present despite that generally being used
to portray the past.

00:24:32.149 --> 00:24:36.420
To me, it kind of portrays the notion that
Jimmy’s life is in general desaturated and

00:24:36.420 --> 00:24:39.020
lifeless and is a husk of himself.

00:24:39.020 --> 00:24:43.860
So not only is the continued story outstanding,
but Vince Gilligan is also making interesting

00:24:43.860 --> 00:24:47.270
creative decisions to make the story even
deeper.

00:24:47.270 --> 00:24:51.520
For the next two topics, I’m going to combine
them since creative visual storytelling and

00:24:51.520 --> 00:24:54.429
the hidden meaning shown through it go hand
in hand.

00:24:54.429 --> 00:24:58.789
I feel like it’s been a while since cinematography
on this caliber always makes me question and

00:24:58.789 --> 00:25:01.960
look into the deeper meaning of what it's
trying to convey.

00:25:01.960 --> 00:25:06.529
Better Call Saul from the blocking of actors,
framing, lighting, scene transitions, and

00:25:06.529 --> 00:25:12.000
more are all extremely deliberate and conveys
some kind of additive message that aids the

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:13.000
script.

00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:16.880
To me this elevates the show because it makes
it significantly more engaging.

00:25:16.880 --> 00:25:21.110
And with engaging cinematography, it makes
you an active participant who is trying to

00:25:21.110 --> 00:25:24.930
pick up on all the small details that these
genius creators leave behind.

00:25:24.930 --> 00:25:29.230
It’s in its rawest form the definition of
show don’t tell in which you want to engage

00:25:29.230 --> 00:25:33.090
the audience mentally with not just words
but every frame.

00:25:33.090 --> 00:25:37.470
Even singular or quick shots can convey so
much information that it essentially adds

00:25:37.470 --> 00:25:39.970
an additional dimension to the storytelling.

00:25:39.970 --> 00:25:44.080
Like let me play this one scene transition
and see if you can spot the deeper meaning

00:25:44.080 --> 00:25:51.059
of what the creators are trying to communicate.

00:25:51.059 --> 00:25:55.590
Now initially this may not seem like anything
major, but given the future events of the

00:25:55.590 --> 00:25:59.860
show where Jimmy and Kim split up, I believe
this is a small nugget of foreshadowing.

00:25:59.860 --> 00:26:04.410
It can’t be a coincidence that we hard cut
from a shot of Jimmy and Kim kissing to someone

00:26:04.410 --> 00:26:06.539
cutting a piece of wood in half.

00:26:06.539 --> 00:26:10.029
Which also happens to be where the two characters
used to be in frame.

00:26:10.029 --> 00:26:13.830
Now for me personally, I knew that Kim and
Jimmy would most likely split up by the end

00:26:13.830 --> 00:26:18.070
of the show, so this scene transition immediately
caught my attention.

00:26:18.070 --> 00:26:23.860
Now even past the usage of creative cinematography,
the creators regularly use metaphors to convey

00:26:23.860 --> 00:26:27.610
certain messages that makes the show even
more thought-provoking.

00:26:27.610 --> 00:26:31.690
The biggest example is the creators putting
a lot of emphasis on the ants devouring this

00:26:31.690 --> 00:26:32.940
ice cream cone.

00:26:32.940 --> 00:26:37.970
For me, I interpret this as Jimmy being the
ice cream cone and the ants being the cartel.

00:26:37.970 --> 00:26:42.950
Jimmy at this point in the story is relatively
innocent and has not yet been fully corrupted.

00:26:42.950 --> 00:26:47.390
Or you can even interpret this past the cartel
and apply it to Jimmy’s entire later life

00:26:47.390 --> 00:26:49.750
of only being surrounded by criminals.

00:26:49.750 --> 00:26:53.800
His innocence as a lawyer and someone who
wants to do some amount of good has completely

00:26:53.800 --> 00:26:55.240
melted away.

00:26:55.240 --> 00:26:59.320
The moment that cements this metaphor is Jimmy’s
reaction to the ice cream cone and us seeing

00:26:59.320 --> 00:27:01.290
his overall dissatisfaction.

00:27:01.290 --> 00:27:06.170
He knows that there is no going back and his
future is going to be very grim.

00:27:06.170 --> 00:27:10.890
Another great example is in the opening of
episode 3 of season 6 where we have this long

00:27:10.890 --> 00:27:16.029
tracking shot that reveals a beautiful blue
flower in the desert alongside a piece of

00:27:16.029 --> 00:27:17.029
glass.

00:27:17.029 --> 00:27:21.310
At first, this doesn’t mean anything to
the viewer until we see the ending and find

00:27:21.310 --> 00:27:24.940
out that this piece of glass is what Nacho
used to escape with.

00:27:24.940 --> 00:27:29.890
So in a heartfelt way, the creators are signifying
that this gorgeous blue flower represents

00:27:29.890 --> 00:27:30.890
Nacho.

00:27:30.890 --> 00:27:35.290
He was the innocent flower amidst the desolate
and miserable wasteland of the drug world.

00:27:35.290 --> 00:27:39.899
It’s a really intelligent moment that rewards
the viewer for paying attention to the details

00:27:39.899 --> 00:27:44.269
of the show, and the second the episode ended
with that final shot, I was kind of blown

00:27:44.269 --> 00:27:45.269
away.

00:27:45.269 --> 00:27:50.120
And you can also interpret the storm in the
beginning as Nacho’s upcoming dark fate.

00:27:50.120 --> 00:27:54.799
Now even going past metaphors, the creators
also use certain objects in scenes and imbue

00:27:54.799 --> 00:27:56.220
them with a certain importance.

00:27:56.220 --> 00:28:02.190
A subtle example that I love is the candle
announcing the arrival of Howard and Lalo.

00:28:02.190 --> 00:28:05.720
Now the reason why I’m bringing this up
is because it’s unnatural to establish an

00:28:05.720 --> 00:28:09.340
element like this when Howard already announces
himself via knocking.

00:28:09.340 --> 00:28:15.940
It’s a redundant piece of information that
instead acts to establish an alternate way

00:28:15.940 --> 00:28:18.440
of announcing the entrance of a character.

00:28:18.440 --> 00:28:22.870
So when it inevitably comes back up again
with Lalo’s sudden entrance, you as a viewer

00:28:22.870 --> 00:28:24.820
immediately know what’s happening.

00:28:24.820 --> 00:28:29.780
Also, this is the part that I love, but if
you’re really paying attention to this unconventional

00:28:29.780 --> 00:28:33.220
shot, it adds a layer of mystery and overall
dread to the scene.

00:28:33.220 --> 00:28:37.659
Because now you’re asking yourself why did
the creators suddenly use this candle and

00:28:37.659 --> 00:28:39.549
will it come back up again.

00:28:39.549 --> 00:28:43.140
Meaning that there could be an additional
surprise character that shows up towards the

00:28:43.140 --> 00:28:45.030
end of the scene.

00:28:45.030 --> 00:28:49.240
Along with the framing of Howard’s entrance
being somewhat mysterious in which he isn’t

00:28:49.240 --> 00:28:53.159
revealed until he’s in the apartment and
all we have is Jimmy’s reaction.

00:28:53.159 --> 00:28:57.179
Which is partially similar to the dread-like
feeling of Lalo entering the apartment where

00:28:57.179 --> 00:28:59.179
he emerges from the shadows.

00:28:59.179 --> 00:29:03.820
This in contrast to the first time Lalo shows
up to the apartment is different because initially

00:29:03.820 --> 00:29:08.700
it was meant as a sudden surprise, while here,
the creators build this uneasy atmosphere

00:29:08.700 --> 00:29:12.620
with Howard’s meltdown and climax it with
Lalo killing him.

00:29:12.620 --> 00:29:16.050
That and there’s so much more to analyze
just with this single scene like with the

00:29:16.050 --> 00:29:17.490
lighting and overall color.

00:29:17.490 --> 00:29:22.429
It’s shot in such a horror-like way of using
atmospheric lighting to establish this anxious

00:29:22.429 --> 00:29:23.429
tone.

00:29:23.429 --> 00:29:27.620
Along with the harsh oranges pushing Howard's
manic realization even further.

00:29:27.620 --> 00:29:31.610
And guess what there’s even more to be analyzed
with this scene through the evolution of the

00:29:31.610 --> 00:29:33.310
blocking, lighting, and editing.

00:29:33.310 --> 00:29:37.500
The scene with Howard starts in which he walks
into the kitchen that is lit much brighter

00:29:37.500 --> 00:29:40.270
and doesn’t communicate this uneasy tone
yet.

00:29:40.270 --> 00:29:44.640
Then as the scene progresses, he positions
himself in front of this dark void in which

00:29:44.640 --> 00:29:46.540
the living room is behind him.

00:29:46.540 --> 00:29:50.830
Through this absence of light, it gives us
less security because of that innate feeling

00:29:50.830 --> 00:29:52.760
of being scared of the dark.

00:29:52.760 --> 00:29:56.070
Like hell, that’s how they brilliantly introduce
Lalo into the scene.

00:29:56.070 --> 00:30:00.309
He starts out as this shadow creeping into
the frame behind Howard which significantly

00:30:00.309 --> 00:30:02.029
adds to the horror.

00:30:02.029 --> 00:30:05.860
Then for the editing, the scene first starts
with using wide shots and over the course

00:30:05.860 --> 00:30:08.550
of it, the shots get tighter and tighter.

00:30:08.550 --> 00:30:12.940
It progresses this feeling of claustrophobia
and essentially ends with a close-up on Howard

00:30:12.940 --> 00:30:17.919
and the second we finally get to this shot,
we immediately know that something is off.

00:30:17.919 --> 00:30:22.350
Because through basic escalation we know that
this closest shot is essentially the climax

00:30:22.350 --> 00:30:26.220
and it’s framed in such a way to keep the
background behind him visible.

00:30:26.220 --> 00:30:30.279
Like sorry if I’m overanalyzing this one
scene, but I think this illustrates the point

00:30:30.279 --> 00:30:34.539
in which there’s an insane amount of subtext
and under the surface subtle storytelling

00:30:34.539 --> 00:30:36.860
that is happening in almost every scene.

00:30:36.860 --> 00:30:40.940
I’m mainly highlighting Howard’s death
scene because I think it’s a perfect combination

00:30:40.940 --> 00:30:42.049
of everything.

00:30:42.049 --> 00:30:46.549
Whether that be through cinematography, editing,
blocking, props, coloring, and more.

00:30:46.549 --> 00:30:50.600
It’s invigorating as a viewer to really
sink your teeth into these scenes because

00:30:50.600 --> 00:30:52.690
there’s so much depth to them.

00:30:52.690 --> 00:30:56.360
Every aspect of filmmaking is operating at
such a high quality that it makes this show

00:30:56.360 --> 00:30:57.809
a true feast.

00:30:57.809 --> 00:31:02.100
Like Vince Gilligan isn’t just a writing
genius, but a filmmaking genius.

00:31:02.100 --> 00:31:05.730
Through and through one of the most brilliant
scenes of a show I watched, and it isn’t

00:31:05.730 --> 00:31:09.809
just great because it was shocking but because
of how genius the creators went about in telling

00:31:09.809 --> 00:31:10.830
it.

00:31:10.830 --> 00:31:14.409
Another scene that really caught my attention
that I want to point out before moving on

00:31:14.409 --> 00:31:18.250
was the way in which they edited Jimmy and
Kim going about their lives while Mike cleaned

00:31:18.250 --> 00:31:19.779
up the apartment.

00:31:19.779 --> 00:31:24.140
Now I’m a sucker for amazing scene transitions
because they can really boost the cleverness

00:31:24.140 --> 00:31:28.570
of your film or show, and the vast majority
of the time filmmakers don’t do anything

00:31:28.570 --> 00:31:30.000
special with them.

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:35.529
However, here, contrasting Mike, Jimmy, and
Kim’s day with match cuts and resolves was

00:31:35.529 --> 00:31:37.299
utterly brilliant.

00:31:37.299 --> 00:31:41.990
Through them cutting between the coffee pouring
into a cup to squeezing out blood into a bucket,

00:31:41.990 --> 00:31:46.030
Mike washing blood off the floor to Jimmy
soaking up the red sauce of his lunch with

00:31:46.030 --> 00:31:50.210
bread, Kim providing evidence of a dead victim
to Mike looking at picture where Howards body

00:31:50.210 --> 00:31:55.110
used to be, every transition in this scene
is infused with so much creativity that it

00:31:55.110 --> 00:31:57.389
makes it incredibly thought-provoking.

00:31:57.389 --> 00:32:01.309
This attention to detail and planning out
these scene transitions elevates the art in

00:32:01.309 --> 00:32:02.309
my opinion.

00:32:02.309 --> 00:32:06.610
It shows how much care the creators are putting
into this scene because this goes past just

00:32:06.610 --> 00:32:07.610
editing.

00:32:07.610 --> 00:32:10.760
The creators have to plan out these shots
and think about the significance of their

00:32:10.760 --> 00:32:13.529
meaning and how it adds more to the story.

00:32:13.529 --> 00:32:18.030
To me, it establishes a genius parallel between
these three characters of juxtaposing their

00:32:18.030 --> 00:32:21.720
ordinary lives to the horrors of what’s
going on in their home.

00:32:21.720 --> 00:32:26.580
It’s brilliant and so are so many other
scenes and shots within this artful show.

00:32:26.580 --> 00:32:31.120
For the last great aspect of Better Call Saul,
I want to highlight how great the subtle acting

00:32:31.120 --> 00:32:32.230
is throughout the show.

00:32:32.230 --> 00:32:36.399
And how the creators use minimalist dialogue
to let the actors really shine and carry the

00:32:36.399 --> 00:32:38.110
narrative through their performances.

00:32:38.110 --> 00:32:42.670
I said I was going to return to Gus Fring
in this section and that’s because I think

00:32:42.670 --> 00:32:45.940
he’s the best example for this subtle form
of acting.

00:32:45.940 --> 00:32:49.930
His deadpan and blunt-like serious delivery
creates a shroud of mystery as to what his

00:32:49.930 --> 00:32:52.360
intentions and motivations are.

00:32:52.360 --> 00:32:56.990
Because of this, we have to really pay attention
to the subtle intricacies of Giancarlo’s

00:32:56.990 --> 00:33:00.460
performance to pick up on the smallest of
details that allude to different aspects of

00:33:00.460 --> 00:33:01.460
the story.

00:33:01.460 --> 00:33:05.769
Like one of my favorite examples being his
talk with Hector Salamanca after the death

00:33:05.769 --> 00:33:07.120
of Lalo.

00:33:07.120 --> 00:33:10.570
Hector as well especially with his added context
through Better Call Saul of his feud with

00:33:10.570 --> 00:33:13.370
Gus, really adds a deeper layer to his acting.

00:33:13.370 --> 00:33:17.260
Every moment you can see Hector’s emotion
pour through his performance despite not being

00:33:17.260 --> 00:33:19.710
able to deliver any lines of dialogue.

00:33:19.710 --> 00:33:23.929
Like his reaction to hearing Lalo’s voice
and transforming his expression from being

00:33:23.929 --> 00:33:26.590
depressed to excited was great.

00:33:26.590 --> 00:33:31.289
But going back to his scene with Gus, I love
the subtle mind games on display here.

00:33:31.289 --> 00:33:36.029
Gus picks up on the small detail of Hector
being partially proud and arrogant when normally

00:33:36.029 --> 00:33:37.700
he hates Gus.

00:33:37.700 --> 00:33:42.470
This signals to Gus that something is wrong,
which means that Lalo is most likely alive.

00:33:42.470 --> 00:33:48.049
From here, Gus bases his entire plan around
Lalo being alive just from this one tiny little

00:33:48.049 --> 00:33:49.049
interaction.

00:33:49.049 --> 00:33:53.070
It’s a genius moment for Gus as a character
that makes him come across as being incredibly

00:33:53.070 --> 00:33:54.519
observant and intelligent.

00:33:54.519 --> 00:33:59.520
I also love the scene after Lalo’s death
in which he gets questioned by Don Eladio.

00:33:59.520 --> 00:34:05.130
This scene is infused with palm-sweating tension
and as an audience, we eagerly watch Giancarlo’s

00:34:05.130 --> 00:34:07.159
performance for any minor tells.

00:34:07.159 --> 00:34:11.440
Subtle moments include Gus trying to hide
his pain when being pat down by the security

00:34:11.440 --> 00:34:16.210
guard, Gus’ long pondering introductions
slightly portraying a nervous tone, when he

00:34:16.210 --> 00:34:20.899
says he has no response there’s a minor
hesitant blink because he’s making a ballsy

00:34:20.899 --> 00:34:25.629
play, then when asked as to if that’s truly
his response to the allegation he reaffirms

00:34:25.629 --> 00:34:29.860
his stance with a more confident delivery,
as the scene progresses and Don Eladio makes

00:34:29.860 --> 00:34:35.070
the case for Gus his stare hardens becoming
even more confident, and then this might also

00:34:35.070 --> 00:34:40.419
be a hidden meaning aspect or me over analyzing,
but when Gus looks at hector there’s a reflection

00:34:40.419 --> 00:34:45.140
of fire in Gus’ glasses which could portray
his fiery hatred towards Hector.

00:34:45.140 --> 00:34:47.149
Or it could just be a coincidence.

00:34:47.149 --> 00:34:51.461
Which again, there’s a lot to be analyzed
in so many scenes in Better Call Saul, but

00:34:51.461 --> 00:34:55.429
going back to the subtle acting point, this
scene is thoroughly engaging because we have

00:34:55.429 --> 00:34:57.599
to pay strict attention to Gus’ performance.

00:34:57.599 --> 00:35:02.859
There are so many subtleties and insinuations
being made, that makes the story of this scene

00:35:02.859 --> 00:35:04.970
so complex and intriguing.

00:35:04.970 --> 00:35:09.900
I know scenes with reams of dialogue and monologues
are clear standouts, but these types of smaller

00:35:09.900 --> 00:35:13.720
moments in which the dialogue falls away and
the performances take the forefront of our

00:35:13.720 --> 00:35:16.730
attention are equally as brilliant.

00:35:16.730 --> 00:35:20.390
That and it’s not just Gus and there’s
a plethora of moments between every character

00:35:20.390 --> 00:35:24.560
in which the writers use minimalist dialogue
to allow the acting of these great actors

00:35:24.560 --> 00:35:26.530
to carry the storytelling.

00:35:26.530 --> 00:35:31.119
Like more examples are Nacho’s final scene
with Lalo where Nacho has to internalize the

00:35:31.119 --> 00:35:35.810
oncoming doom of everyone’s death in the
compound, almost any scene in the present

00:35:35.810 --> 00:35:39.740
with Jimmy, Kim’s boiling anger towards
Jimmy when he defects and goes against her

00:35:39.740 --> 00:35:44.590
wishes during the Mesa Verde problem, Lalo
coldly considering Jimmy’s proposal to send

00:35:44.590 --> 00:35:49.790
Kim to kill Gus which drastically increased
the tension, and almost every Mike scene since

00:35:49.790 --> 00:35:52.810
his usage of dialogue is meticulous and efficient.

00:35:52.810 --> 00:35:57.130
The acting in Better Call Saul is outstanding
and it’s unfortunate that Succession will

00:35:57.130 --> 00:36:00.010
most likely win in every category it's nominated
in.

00:36:00.010 --> 00:36:04.750
Our only true hope is that Rhea Seehorn wins
for best supporting actress because to me

00:36:04.750 --> 00:36:06.829
she is the best in that category.

00:36:06.829 --> 00:36:10.960
I know I’m titling this video as being the
best show of 2023 and that should in turn

00:36:10.960 --> 00:36:15.380
invite a compare and contrast between Succession
and Better Call Saul, but both of these shows

00:36:15.380 --> 00:36:16.970
are easily 10/10s.

00:36:16.970 --> 00:36:19.609
Along with them being pretty different types
of shows.

00:36:19.609 --> 00:36:24.580
So it’s obviously off of your personal preference
and I just wanted to make a case for why Better

00:36:24.580 --> 00:36:28.460
Call Saul is the best show because I feel
like it deserves more praise.

00:36:28.460 --> 00:36:32.440
It’s one of the most brilliant shows I’ve
ever seen and thank you so much for watching

00:36:32.440 --> 00:36:34.440
this entire lengthy video essay.

00:36:34.440 --> 00:36:38.650
I didn’t intend on it going this long, but
it would have felt like a disservice to the

00:36:38.650 --> 00:36:40.040
show making it any shorter.

00:36:40.040 --> 00:36:43.329
Let me know in the comments what you think
about Better Call and Saul and my socials

00:36:43.329 --> 00:36:44.760
are linked in the description.

00:36:44.760 --> 00:36:49.490
And again, thank you so much to my top patron
Logan Farmer.

00:36:49.490 --> 00:36:52.910
I can’t express how thankful I am for you
hitting the highest tier.

